study/style-library/techniques-transitions.md:329:### T233 — Tool transformation: pincushion tomato through the grid press (P13 T10) ◆ — OWED #104
study/style-library/techniques-transitions.md:330:- Tier: **DESCRIBED — OWED #104**
    65	
    66	### T09 — Verb literalization: dicing produces dice
    67	- **Timestamps:** 29–36 s (S3) · **Clip:** clips/baseball-dicing.mp4 (28–36 s)
    68	- **Mechanics:** The slices are gathered and — at the moment the knife begins to dice them — they are a small stack of white dice. Successive chops multiply the dice: each pass lands more, smaller dice, until the board carries a pile of tiny dice, which the hands gather like chopped onion. Two mechanisms are coded separately (P-A4): the initial CASTING (baseball as onion, T07–T08) and the LITERALIZATION — the verb "to dice" made visible as its homonym. The multiplication is an intra-shot change: visible object-state changes with no shot boundary claimed.
    69	- **Why it works:** the pun is executed as procedure, not caption — the viewer watches the word happen, which is why it survives translation to any language.
    70	- **Evidence:** ev/t032-dice-stack.jpg (the slices landed as a stack of dice under the knife) · ev/t035-tiny-dice.jpg (the multiplied pile of tiny dice on the board) · ev/t038-dice-sprinkle.jpg (the tiny dice sprinkled into the molcajete over the green flesh)
    71	
    72	### T10 — Tool transformation: pincushion tomato through the grid press
    73	- **Timestamps:** 39–46 s (S4) · **Clip:** clips/pincushion-grid.mp4 (41–46 s)
    74	- **Mechanics:** A tomato-red pincushion is de-pinned (its pins read as the tomato's stem-points), set into a grid cutter, and pressed through; what emerges is a slab of translucent red cubes — diced tomato — gathered by hand and added to the molcajete. The transformation is performed by a real kitchen tool doing its real job; the substitution rides inside the press's occlusion.
    75	- **Why it works:** the tool's function is the argument — a grid cutter can only output cubes, so the output's identity is forced by the instrument, not by the object's resemblance.
    76	- **Evidence:** ev/t041-pincushion-tomato.jpg (pincushion in hand, pins visible) · ev/t044-grid-press.jpg (the grid cutter pressing the pincushion)
    77	
    78	### T11 — Halving reveal + press: the golf-ball lime
    79	- **Timestamps:** 46–54 s (S5) · **Clip:** clips/golf-lime-press.mp4 (48–54 s)
    80	- **Mechanics:** A green golf ball rolls onto the board and is stopped by a palm (its dimples read as lime peel at this scale and colour). Halved, its cut face is a lime's cross-section — segments and pith drawn by the reveal. One half goes into a metal citrus press and is squeezed over the molcajete; the press's handles and leverage are the real tool's.
    81	- **Why it works:** two reveals are chained — the halving proves the casting, the pressing spends it — so the episode teaches that a cast object must survive being used, not just being seen.
   128	## 5. Goods and bads
   129	### Goods
   130	- **G1 (7–14 s):** The first casting is taught slowly and completely — selection, halving, reveal — before any faster episode spends the viewer's fluency.
   131	- **G2 (29–36 s):** The dicing literalization (T09) is a wordless pun executed as pure procedure; it needs no caption in any language.
   132	- **G3 (16–21 s, recurring):** The molcajete's accumulating contents (T06) give a sequence of gags a single visible state — the recipe is never in doubt.
   133	- **G4 (41–46 s):** The grid press (T10) lets a real tool force the transformation; instrument-forced identity is more convincing than resemblance alone.
   134	- **G5 (75–81 s):** The mash (T15) spends every casting at once and converts the trick into food — the payoff is procedural, not a final gag.
   135	- **G6 (89–94 s):** The chip break in the dip (T16) is the film's one allowed physical failure, and it certifies the physical honesty of everything before it.
   136	### Bads
   137	- **B1 (95–100.9 s):** The end cards hold the final ~6 s of the 100.9 s runtime in bookend stillness, and the closing PES card repeats information the opening already gave.
   138	- **B2 (67–75 s):** The pawn seasoning (T14) asserts its product by gesture alone — nothing visibly falls — the one casting the film does not evidence; a first-time viewer can miss that seasoning happened at all.
   139	- **B3 (54–57 s):** The tree shot (T12) is the only flatly lit, grey-wall composition in a warm wooden film; the location change reads as a different production for its first second.
   140	- **B4 (24–29 s):** The slicing beat (T08) is the longest stretch with no transformation (≈5 s); priced as restraint in §3, it is also where the film's pulse sags before the dice payoff.
   141	- **B5 (39–41 s):** The de-pinning of the pincushion is fumbled in small hand movements at the edge of legibility — the pins' removal, which carries the tomato reading, is the least readable action in the film at normal speed.
   142	- **B6 (0–4 s):** The opening holds two cards (PES, then title) before the sharpening ritual — four seconds of black before the film's world appears is a slow door for a 101 s piece.
   143	
   144	## 6. Lessons mapped to our containers
   145	| # | Lesson | Class | Container | Change it implies | Cost |
   146	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   147	| L1 | Substitution-at-action: swap the object inside a performed action whose real outcome predicts the reveal; record before/after/spanning action/boundary-visibility as separate evidence fields | technique | transition | Add a substitution transition type to the assembler: action spans the swap; the incoming object's identity is the action's expected product | moderate — needs before/after asset pairs per use, but the container logic is one rule |
   148	| L2 | Object casting by shape + colour + context, asserted by handling (selecting, hefting), never by label | technique | transition · diagram | Diagram objects may be cast by resemblance and used in role without an explanatory caption | cheap — a casting rule, not new machinery |
   149	| L3 | Verb literalization: when the spoken verb has a concrete homonym or product-shape, the visual can land as that product (dicing → dice) | trick | word animation | Word-animation containers gain a literalization beat: the word's action produces its literal object on its onset | moderate — per-word asset, but the beat structure is reusable |
   150	| L4 | Casting and literalization are separate mechanisms and must be planned separately: an object is first cast in a role, then an action may re-read it | trick | transition · word animation | Transition plans carry two fields (cast-as · re-read-as) instead of one "transformation" note | cheap — a plan-field change |
